 We have seen also that models are getting really, really good, so it's not a problem of the models, but it's a problem of the operational gap.

30
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:48,340
 So there is no observability, poor evaluations, poor governance, and all of this is then blocking those prototypes to go to the next level.

31
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:02,120
 So that's like the context why we are having this talk today, and what we will go to cover is just like how we go from DevOps to Agent Ops.

32
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:27,180
 Also, what are the common challenges we see in the industry, what are the solutions we have been practicing, and we see that work as well with customers, and go back to those principles that we have applied in traditional software development, and that are also still evolving, but very valid and present in the agent era.

33
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:30,300
 So, yeah.

34
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:50,780
 So, we have in class in development, software development, the DevOps principles, so I'm not sure if you are familiar with the handbook of DevOps, and there are three main ways, the way of flow, the way of feedback, and the way of continuous learning.

35
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:59,620
 So, flow is all about these very quick changes and deliveries of software, but in a reliable and safe way.

36
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:16,380
 And then we have the feedback, where we are actually monitoring, testing, and gathering all these signals, and then in the continuous learning, we learn from errors, and iterate those test suites, the architecture, the patterns we are using.

37
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:43,420
 And that was really well, and it has also been evolving to the DevOps and the Agent Ops, where I'm not sure if we have many data scientists here in the audience, but in the DevOps, we have like nearly three personas, so the data engineer, the data scientist, and then the ML engineer, and the idea was everything was centered around the model.

38
00:06:43,420 --> 00:07:10,080
 So, we will train a model, so we will train a model, we will train a model, we will train a model, we will train a model, we will train a model, we will train a model, and those metrics were quite deterministic, so if it was classification, like decision, recall, accuracy, if it was a forecasting model, or a regression model, so around the curve, and so on, so it was quite, not easy, but quite, yeah, defined, and like a limited scope of how we measure it.

39
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:19,420
 So, then we have like, then we have like a, also, quite fixed behavior, so it will be like classification, or it will give you like an answer, right?

40
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:29,420
 And also, you will test, once in production, if the model will show like drifting, due to the model behavior, or to the data.

41
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:38,420
 So, it was complex, but now, with the agentic era, we have not a model, but we have a compound system.

42
00:07:38,420 --> 00:07:54,420
 So, we have there models, prompts, tools, the context, we need to do different routine, we need to ensure that we have guardraps in place, so lots of little components all together in this composed model architecture.

43
00:07:54,420 --> 00:08:10,420
 Now, we don't have a model giving you an answer, but we have an agent making decisions based on the particular input, and those decisions, because we have the stochastic nature of LLMs, can be, can be changing, right?

44
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:17,420
 So, you need to be like, throw the whole harness to ensure that it is doing what you are expecting it to do.

45
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:24,420
 And because we are talking about natural language, it's very difficult to test and measure and evaluate as well.

46
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:36,420
 So, you don't have this nice decision to collect some metrics, you now have to think about, is this relevant? Is this following a particular tone? Is this toxic?

47
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:43,420
 There are lots of different aspects you need to take into consideration when evaluating this kind of agentic solutions.

48
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:53,420
 As I mentioned, behavior is different, so it changes at runtime depending on what it is deciding to do.

49
00:08:53,420 --> 00:09:05,420
 Because it's taking actions, it's quite dangerous, so if you are talking with a chatbot, and it starts advertising the wrong product, or giving you the wrong information, that's really bad for your company.

50
00:09:05,420 --> 00:09:23,420
 And now we don't have these three personas only, but we have lots of people involved, because it's like, you can have like the product owners, the SMEs, you still have data scientists, we have now software engineers building these agentic solutions.

51
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:33,420
 It's lots of personas, and all of them with different objectives, so it's more difficult and challenging to manage as projects.

52
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:57,420
 And what are the common challenges we have seen from this evolution, is that, one, is controlling governance, so before it was about who can access the model, now it's like, who can access the agent, and what can you do with this agent?

53
00:09:57,420 --> 00:10:11,420
 So, usually what we have seen in a lot of customers, is that they run these agents, with a service principle that maybe has lots of permissions, much more permissions than a user should have.

54
00:10:11,420 --> 00:10:17,420
 So, this is one of the common things we see, and that can create some security and vulnerability issues.

55
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:43,420
 Then we have, because everyone is super excited about this, everyone is building tools, building agents, building MCPs, and skills, and if you don't have like a managed way to have like a registry or something to have control of that, you will have lots of overlap and agent explore, which is like super messy, and then you will have things just building the same thing again and again.

56
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:58,420
 And then guardrails, so they have been neglected in many first implementations, and then later on when you are in production, or going into production, you notice that they were like really badly needed.

57
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:02,420
 So, this is like a commonly stable charge we have seen.

58
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:19,420
 Then in terms of observability and feedback, it's important to have like everything traceable observable, because as we have this composed system with so many bits moving around, you cannot just observe input and output.

59
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:26,420
 Because sometimes the output is quite confident, but it's wrong, and it's important to know where it went wrong.

60
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:28,420
 Was it contacting the wrong sub-agent?

61
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:32,420
 Was it contacting the trying to get information from the wrong tool?

62
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:34,420
 Was it missing context?

63
00:11:34,420 --> 00:11:39,420
 So if you don't have that observability, it's just like a black box to imagine.

64
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:46,420
 It's just like a graphic, and feedback is elsewhere, so this is about, now we have different personas, right?

65
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:58,420
 You can run your evaluations in development, then you have your subject matter experts giving you feedback, which is super valuable because it's the human expert feedback, you can get to your solutions.

66
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:07,420
 And then once you are in production, for instance, you have these counter-apps done, or you can provide feedback regarding the answer you received.

67
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:13,420
 And if you don't have like a consolidated way to get all of this feedback, it's just like wasted feedback.

68
00:12:13,420 --> 00:12:16,420
 That is very, very important to improve your model.

69
00:12:16,420 --> 00:12:32,420
 And then the final one, I think everyone is super concerned about this now, is that tokens are expensive, and especially like C-level executives, they want to see the return of investment.

70
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:35,420
 And it's very important to be able to track this.

71
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:37,420
 And it's challenging.

72
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:43,420
 Another challenge we see that is very common is the relevability and the evaluations gap.

73
00:12:43,420 --> 00:12:54,420
 So, relevability, because now we have these multi-steps decisions and processes to get to that output.

74
00:12:54,420 --> 00:13:03,420
 When evaluating, maybe we see, oh, single step data is doing amazing, but then we will have to do the multiple reasoning steps.

75
00:13:03,420 --> 00:13:05,420
 There might be some errors.

76
00:13:05,420 --> 00:13:15,420
 And if you are not evaluating this correctly, you might get like misleading impression about the quality of the solution.

77
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:26,420
 Then, as we discussed, like we have this stochastic system, so unless you don't have like a really good hardness, you will have like, every time you ask one plus one, maybe you will have different answers.

78
00:13:26,420 --> 00:13:37,420
 But for more complex questions, you might get different answers to the same question, so we need to avoid that.

79
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:47,420
 And also, initially, with so many people just doing bite checks and get super excited about the bite checking, like, oh, yes, it's doing what I want and so on.

80
00:13:47,420 --> 00:14:04,420
 And that's great for development. But then, if you go to the vision of a production scale solution, you need to consider also all the edge cases, the bad situations, which are very difficult to cover in just bite checking.

81
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:22,420
 And last but not least, the maintenance, and this is like the result of, how do you maintain all of this that you have not controlled, governed, you cannot observe, you cannot trace, you don't know how much it's costing.

82
00:14:22,420 --> 00:14:33,420
 So it's like a compounded debt, that is very expensive if you don't try to, like, consider about this from the start.

83
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:43,420
 And then, so those are like the main challenges. So what kind of solutions have we seen that are working and are the trend now?

84
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:56,420
 So, first of all, in these solutions, we have several levels of PCT. There are, like, simple automations that is just, like, doing a call to the LLM with a instruction and that's it.

85
00:14:56,420 --> 00:15:04,420
 So that's, like, level one super simple. Then we have the second level is when you have, like, what you used to know as track.

86
00:15:04,420 --> 00:15:13,420
 You go and have the retrieval, and based on the retrieval, you craft the answer, the response to the question.

87
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:25,420
 The next level is, besides that context, you give also tools, MCP servers, in order to either gather more information or trigger actions.

88
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:33,420
 And then the last level, to be honest, we don't have many customers doing this yet, because it's quite difficult to, to maintain it.

89
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:38,420
 So, you know, the first thing that is, like, something that's not necessary for the use case is the multi-agent.

90
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:48,420
 So here you have either a supervisor with several agents, where you can, based on the tasks, just, like, assign a task to the agent.

91
00:15:48,420 --> 00:15:54,420
 Or you have several agents talking with each other via A2A or something similar.

92
00:15:54,420 --> 00:16:07,420
 And that's what very complex solutions, where you need, like, multiple actions, and you need, like, alignment among the agents too.

93
00:16:07,420 --> 00:16:10,420
 So, how do you remind those complexities?

94
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:20,420
 We can see, like, five operational controls that we can have across all these layers of complexity we have seen in the architectures.

95
00:16:20,420 --> 00:16:29,420
 And they are, like, tracing, that's for the observability, because now we need to check, like, what is doing each tool, like, each code the LL is doing.

96
00:16:29,420 --> 00:16:30,420
 So, what is happening with that?

97
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:31,420
 That's important.

98
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:34,420
 And, um, evaluation gates.

99
00:16:34,420 --> 00:16:39,420
 So, here is what I was mentioning, build this golden data set.

100
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:42,420
 Similar as with any ML project.

101
00:16:42,420 --> 00:16:50,420
 You need to have this very nice distribution of good, bad, and very seldom use cases of what is expected output.

102
00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:58,420
 Governance, yeah, deployment and rollback strategies, and how you are going to monitor this.

103
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:04,420
 And for the first architecture, it's very easy, it's very similar to emerging solutions.

104
00:17:04,420 --> 00:17:14,420
 You have just, like, tracing of the prompt and the input output tokens, how long is it taking, the golden data sets we mentioned already.

105
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:22,420
 You are going in the model at the prompt, and deployment is, like, quick rollback, you can do canary deployment as well.

106
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:28,420
 And the monitoring will be just, like, the response drift and great limits, right?

107
00:17:28,420 --> 00:17:36,420
 In the second architecture, the next level, it's a little bit more complex, because now you have that retriever, so you have to trace as well,

108
00:17:36,420 --> 00:17:40,420
 Is it going to the right vector search index?

109
00:17:40,420 --> 00:17:43,420
 Is it retriever the right information?

110
00:17:43,420 --> 00:17:45,420
 So, is it grounded?

111
00:17:45,420 --> 00:17:47,420
 Can you decide where it is coming from?

112
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:51,420
 Which chunk of information was it using in order to prompt the response?

113
00:17:51,420 --> 00:17:59,420
 Governance, now we have this vector search index, and the whole pipeline to maintain it, and up to date, so that's another thing to govern.

114
00:17:59,420 --> 00:18:09,420
 And the deployment as well, we have this new element in order to consider it, and then for the monitoring, we just, like, have that next level as well.

115
00:18:09,420 --> 00:18:12,420
 So, it's a little bit more complex.

116
00:18:12,420 --> 00:18:25,420
 For tool coding, it gets more complex, because now we have more tools, more components to double-check, to test, you have to evaluate, to be sure that each tool is doing the right thing,

117
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:34,420
 that the agent is able to understand what those tools are doing, and then able to route the request accordingly.

118
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:47,420
 You need to ensure that there are, like, the right permissions as well for those tools, and if you are doing the canary deployment, you have to consider, okay, what do I, how do I want to do it?

119
00:18:47,420 --> 00:18:53,420
 It's just like, what sets of tools I want to prove in this deployment, and so on.

120
00:18:53,420 --> 00:19:06,420
 So, it gets, like, level of complexity is a little bit higher, and then also the monitoring gets about the tools that you are leveraging, if they are failing, do we have photo alerts, and so forth.

121
00:19:06,420 --> 00:19:23,420
 And then the last one, because you have several agents, you have distributed traces, so you should be able to discern which agent is doing what trace, and what set of tools that particular agents are using,

122
00:19:23,420 --> 00:19:36,420
 the cross-agent identities, so again, like, managing what can they access or not, the deployment, if you have, like, an orchestrator,

123
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:46,420
 they create those orchestrator gates, have this per-agent versioning, and then, like, a snapshot of what the versions that orchestrator will be handling,

124
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:59,420
 and then, for the monitoring, like, change if there are many loops, if there are, like, escalation gates, what are the cost per role, so it gets quite, quite complicated.

125
00:19:59,420 --> 00:20:12,420
 So, in terms of that control and governance questions we have as the first challenge, one recommendation is to have that on behalf of the user,

126
00:20:12,420 --> 00:20:22,420
 instead of having the service principle, who knows how much of the access, that you are not aware of, or, and that the agent doesn't need to have,

127
00:20:22,420 --> 00:20:30,420
 so, that's one thing, the other one is, regarding this agent's role, like, tool is probably to have, like, a government registry,

128
00:20:30,420 --> 00:20:39,420
 that, once, one helps to democratize, as well, like, the access to tools, and so on, to your, your teams and organizations,

129
00:20:39,420 --> 00:20:47,420
 and two, it's much easier to maintain, also, things that are not used, that are not useful, then you can just, like, remove them from the registry,

130
00:20:47,420 --> 00:20:56,420
 and people know that that's the case. You can also, like, control permissions, who has access to what, and they have the guardrails in place,

131
00:20:56,420 --> 00:21:06,420
 and that will be, like, run-time policy on every action, so, are we allowed this action, are we denied it, or do we need a human approval in order to close it?

132
00:21:06,420 --> 00:21:13,420
 And that will ensure that your agent is there, doing the right thing, or at least limiting the options, to avoid errors.

133
00:21:13,420 --> 00:21:25,420
 And here, we have, like, an example of that traceability we were talking about. So, on the left side, we have, like, this input-output log,

134
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:40,420
 where we see, okay, the user was asking about why they were charged twice, and the LLM answers with, like, a very convincing answer, but it's completely wrong.

135
00:21:40,420 --> 00:21:49,420
 So, maybe it was passing the formatting, the right tone, and so on, but it's wrong. And we cannot see here why it was wrong.

136
00:21:49,420 --> 00:21:59,420
 But then, if we are tracing what the LLM is doing, and what the tools have, is it trying to get, and so on, we can see that, when it was trying to get the invoices,

137
00:21:59,420 --> 00:22:11,420
 that tool that tool was timing out, so it doesn't know the latest status, so it goes to a cache of that tool, which was stale,

138
00:22:11,420 --> 00:22:17,420
 so it was not having the right information for the customer, and therefore it was giving the wrong answer.

139
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:29,420
 So, if you don't have that trace, it's very difficult to know. And then, going back into that evaluation and observability,

140
00:22:29,420 --> 00:22:37,420
 we have, like, several, again, quite complex, several sources. So, we have our, like, deterministic checks,

141
00:22:37,420 --> 00:22:46,420
 like, as we used to have in, like, normal software development, where you can check, like, the format, the policy rules,

142
00:22:46,420 --> 00:22:54,420
 PII patterns, like, these are, like, very, very useful, and I highly encourage you to keep using them as, like,

143
00:22:54,420 --> 00:23:00,420
 use as much deterministic approaches as possible, because they are very cheap and quick to run.

144
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:08,420
 And then, you can run it in, run them in every request, in your CI and in production, and it won't, like, cost you much,

145
00:23:08,420 --> 00:23:13,420
 and it will bring you a lot of value about how is the quality of the solution.

146
00:23:13,420 --> 00:23:19,420
 Then, we have the LLM judges. Here, there are different schools of talks. I find them very useful,

147
00:23:19,420 --> 00:23:29,420
 but it depends a lot on what kind of judges you are using. And they are expensive because you are hitting an LLM in order to get that,

148
00:23:29,420 --> 00:23:35,420
 and they are slow. So, slow in comparison, slower in comparison with the deterministic ones.

149
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:40,420
 So, the idea here is use them when you are doing your CI process on your golden beta set,

150
00:23:40,420 --> 00:23:47,420
 so that you are super sure that every change was tested and it's not, like, two integrations and so on.

151
00:23:47,420 --> 00:23:54,420
 But the production is very expensive to run, so you need to have, like, a sampling of your production traffic

152
00:23:54,420 --> 00:24:02,420
 that you will evaluate with those LLM judges. The most helpful one is the domain experts feedback,

153
00:24:02,420 --> 00:24:07,420
 and this is something that you need to have in your projects, in your solutions.

154
00:24:07,420 --> 00:24:13,420
 But this is, like, people are very difficult to just get the bandwidth in order to do that,

155
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:18,420
 so this is something you can do sporadically. At the beginning, it's important to have it once,

156
00:24:18,420 --> 00:24:22,420
 until you get to that good quality, good enough to get to production.

157
00:24:22,420 --> 00:24:29,420
 But then, once in production, you need also to keep them involved, like, with a review from time to time,

158
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:36,420
 quarterly, mostly, depends on your solution, based on what you see from the output in production.

159
00:24:36,420 --> 00:24:42,420
 Judge alignment is about particularly when you have very complex agentic solutions.

160
00:24:42,420 --> 00:24:51,420
 Let's say, for instance, a solution that is providing assistance to a user based on their medical information.

161
00:24:51,420 --> 00:25:00,420
 So, it has to be very, very careful about the sensitivity of the data, what kind of recommendations it does, and so on.

162
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:08,420
 So, even humans evaluating this sometimes cannot get aligned to what is wrong or what is right.

163
00:25:08,420 --> 00:25:14,420
 So, you need to have that phase of aligning what is wrong and right with the human experts,

164
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:18,420
 and then propagate that to your LLM judges.

165
00:25:18,420 --> 00:25:23,420
 And then, end users is about the thumbs up, down, feedback you get from the product.

166
00:25:23,420 --> 00:25:30,420
 So, the idea is that you need to get all of these sources of information, which are like gold for your solution,

167
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:37,420
 and try to get it consolidated in one data model, so that you can action on them.

168
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:52,420
 So, another, like best practice that we have identified and seen, like working in the industry, is like having drive alerts.

169
00:25:52,420 --> 00:26:01,420
 So, before we had alerts on, oh, it is up, it is running, how long is it taking, did we get any particular errors, right?

